<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1976 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Dap Prampi Mesa Moha Chouk Chey</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="km">ដប់ប្រាំពីរមេសាមហាជោគជ័យ</bdi> / <bdi lang="km-Latn">Dap Prampi Mesa Moha Chouk Chey</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>The anthem of the Khmer Rouge government that ruled Cambodia harshly from 1975 to 1979.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>not known (said to be the Khmer Rouge or Pol Pot)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Democratic Kampuchea (the Khmer Rouge state)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Khmer</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Cambodia</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>national anthem from 5 January 1976; perhaps in use earlier</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (anthem of 1976, author unknown)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Dap Prampi Mesa Moha Chokchey&quot; (&quot;The Glorious Seventeenth of April&quot;) was the national anthem of Democratic Kampuchea, the state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a>. Its title refers to 17 April 1975, the day the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> Rouge came to power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and set up a communist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> of Maoist inspiration under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot">Pol Pot</a>. The anthem may have been in use in the Khmer Rouge's &quot;liberated zone&quot; much earlier, but it was proclaimed the national anthem by article 18 of the Constitution of Kampuchea, promulgated on 5 January 1976. The Khmer Rouge, or Pol Pot himself, is supposed to have written it, but its origin remains unknown.</p><p>The regime emptied the cities and marched Cambodians to labour camps in the countryside, with mass executions and forced labour: by January 1979, 1.5 to 2 million people, a fifth to a quarter of the population of 1975, had died as a result of its policies, in what is known as the Cambodian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. From 1977 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pol-pot">Pol Pot</a> also carried out a series of purges and had many anti-personnel mines laid, especially along the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, and relations with Vietnam broke down. In 1979 Vietnamese forces invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> and drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a> from most of the country; the People's Republic of Kampuchea that they set up adopted a new anthem.</p><p>The Coalition Government of Democratic Kampuchea, formed in exile, kept &quot;Dap Prampi Mesa Moha Chokchey&quot; as its state anthem. Because many Western nations and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> recognised that government as the legitimate government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a>, its anthem went on being presented in the West as the national anthem of Cambodia until the monarchy was restored in 1993.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A state anthem celebrating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-rouge">Khmer Rouge</a>'s seizure of power on 17 April 1975 and its revolution; martial and exultant in tone.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so they are not printed here. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above gives their theme in our own words.</p></section>
</div>
